# Work Order No. 1851-07 -- bench answers. Fill in after each colon.
# Copy me first, from labs/week-07:   cp check/answers-template check/answers.txt
# The seal checks (make -C check m1 ... m3) read check/answers.txt -- this
# template stays untouched. Lines starting with # are notes, not answers.
#
# Keep each answer on its ONE labelled line. Every check reads the line that
# starts with its label and nothing else, so a second line of working notes
# is fine here but will not be read.
#
# Where a slot wants more than one number, put them all on the one line,
# with SPACES between them. A comma is read as part of a figure (120,000 and
# 120000 are one number, everywhere), so 07,21 would be read as 0721.
# Box names may be written however you like to type them -- Old Quarter,
# old-quarter and OLDQUARTER are read as one name, everywhere.

# A1 (Task 1) -- the SECONDS of each of the four holds in the acquisition
# log: the last two digits of every "hold taken" time. Four numbers, any
# order; a leading zero is fine either way. E.g.:
#   A1: NN NN NN NN
A1: 07 21 35 49

# A2 (Task 1) -- the gap between one hold and the next, in whole seconds.
# One number, worked out from the log yourself, and nothing else on the
# line that looks like a number.
A2: 14

# A3 (Task 1) -- the ONE box the manual page says a release order should
# name, spelled as the log spells it. The box, not the rule.
A3: Kiln Row

# A4 (Task 2) -- the ring, from your own backtraces, as a chain of waits
# that goes all the way round and back to where it started:
#   A4: X waits for Y, Y waits for Z, Z waits for W, W waits for X
# "Waits for" means: X's take_lever frame is asking for the lever that Y
# holds. Any box may come first; the direction is what matters. Only the
# chain goes on this line; asides and reasons go in your logbook.
A4: Northgate waits for Waterside, Waterside waits for Old Quarter, Old Quarter waits for Kiln Row, Kiln Row waits for Northgate

# A5 (Task 2) -- the line number gdb reports for the waiting lock: the
# number after "ring-frame.c:" in any take_lever frame. Not a frame number
# (after the #), not a thread number, not an LWP. One number.
A5: 127

# A6a to A6d (Task 3) -- the four conditions for deadlock, ONE to a line.
# Each line starts with the condition's name, then carries its evidence
# from your own gdb screen on the SAME line: the command, and what it
# printed that shows the condition holding. Any order will do; the order
# Task 3 teaches them in is the easiest. E.g.:
#   A6a: <condition> -- <command>: <what it showed>
A6a: Mutual exclusion -> print lever_northgate: the mutex is locked and has one owner.
A6b: Hold and wait -> thread apply all bt: each district holds one lever while waiting for another.
A6c: No preemption -> thread apply all bt: the waiting threads cannot take a lever away from the thread that holds it.
A6d: Circular wait -> info threads: Northgate waits for Waterside, Waterside waits for Old Quarter, Old Quarter waits for Kiln Row, and Kiln Row waits for Northgate.

# A7 (Task 3) -- the line the frame printed when it honoured your release
# order. Copy it whole, exactly as it came out.
A7:

# A8 (Task 3) -- the ONE condition the release broke, by the name Task 3
# gives it, alone on the line. Why it is that one, and not another, goes
# in your logbook.
A8:
